title: Navigation
tags: [[Rollback]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: Moving HEAD from one operation to another — through their lowest common ancestor, undoing then re-applying — atomically or one step at a time, and what the daemon owes afterwards.

Given the current HEAD and a target:

# find their ''lowest common ancestor'';
# the ''unapply path'': from HEAD up to it (excluded), newest first;
# the ''apply path'': from it down to the target (excluded), oldest first;
# execute: each unapplied operation's inverse, then each applied operation's forward action, then move HEAD to the target.

The ''atomic'' form (`POST /rollback`) does it all in one transaction: a crash leaves HEAD
and the data as they were. The ''coordinated'' form does it one operation per
transaction, so that the client can carry out each step's file action
([[Filesystem coordination]]). A target of "nothing" (rolling back past the root) empties
the repository.

!! Inverses

|!Operation |!Inverse |
|`create_metarecord` |delete the metarecord and its rows |
|`delete_metarecord` |recreate it from the snapshots; its version follows the restored rows |
|`set_field` |delete the current rows of the cell; insert the before rows under their original ids |
|`append_field` |delete the row whose id the after snapshot names |
|`delete_field` |insert the before rows under their original ids |
|`file_deleted`, `file_moved` |replace the current `mfr_path` row by the before rows, original ids |
|`file_modified` |replace `mfr_size`, `mfr_mtime` and the hash rows by the before rows |
|`unknown` |impossible — navigating before one is refused with `400` |

Rows come back under their original ids, and each touched metarecord's version is
recomputed from them ([[Log storage]]). Navigation writes rows directly, not through a
`Writer`, and validates nothing: it restores states the log recorded as valid.

!! Upkeep after a navigation

A navigation restores `mf_watch`/`mf_ignore` rows like any others — rolling back the
deletion of a tracked directory brings its tracking back — so the daemon owes the same
follow-up as a write: the watch set is recomputed against the state HEAD landed on
([[When watch or ignore changes]]). ''Once HEAD has landed'' is the whole rule: at the end
of an atomic navigation; at the last step of a coordinated one, or at `abort` (which keeps
the state it stopped at) — never in between, where each step is a fragment of a revision
and the states passed need not be consistent. Without it, a repository watched nothing
after a rollback that restored its tracking, until some unrelated write touched a rule.

And, as after a write, ''only when an operation taken back or applied moved a rule'' — its
field is `mf_watch`, `mf_ignore` or `mfr_watch_exceeded`, or the metarecord it created,
deleted or set whole held one (a coordinated navigation remembers it from step to step).
Recomputing walks every watched folder on disk: a rollback that took back a tag paid it on
every call, 70 ms on a repository of a thousand folders where the navigation itself cost
ten (found by the API sweep of [[The timed regression suite]]).

The watcher stays live throughout; its events are held until the navigation ends
([[Filesystem coordination]]).

Why a step costs the operation it applies, and not the log: [[What undoing used to cost]].
